module gunbc.runner.runner_harness_refusal_receipt

import std.types { Bool, Int, List, NonEmptyStr, String }

// THE HARNESS ARM THAT HAD NEVER FIRED, NOW FIRED ON PURPOSE.
//
// gunbc.runner.runner_bound_attempt_receipt recorded it as written and unexercised: every run since
// the change had booted on its first attempt or hit a firmware error, so the arm that refuses on an
// AMBIGUOUS reading had no evidence at all. An unexercised refusal is the one this lane has learned
// least to trust -- it is indistinguishable from a refusal that cannot fire.

// ── why the arm exists ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// THE HARNESS ONCE POWER-CYCLED A MACHINE RUNNING A LIVE CI JOB. A console the BMC had closed read
// as a failed boot, so it cycled -- destroying the only observation of the run it existed to record,
// and producing the capture that was then published under the wrong identity.
//
// THE DEFECT WAS NOT THE MISSED LINE. It was that an ambiguous reading triggered a DESTRUCTIVE
// action: the observer could not distinguish "I stopped watching" from "it stopped working", so it
// chose the arm that resolved the ambiguity in its own favour and charged the cost to whatever was
// running.
data arm_under_test: NonEmptyStr = "only a firmware error on a LIVE console authorises another power cycle; any other reading stops and tells the operator to look"

// ── the first attempt to exercise it FAILED, and that is the more useful finding ───────────────

// HOLDING THE SOL SESSION DID NOT PRODUCE AMBIGUITY. The intended setup was to occupy the BMC's
// single console session so the harness would see nothing. It saw the console perfectly: the
// harness's own `sol deactivate` reclaims the session before activating, so a competing holder is
// simply evicted.
//
// SO THE STATE IS NOT REACHABLE BY CONTENTION, which is worth knowing independently of the test. A
// dropped console on this BMC comes from the controller's own session handling, not from another
// client taking it -- meaning the failure that destroyed a live run cannot be reproduced by racing
// for the console, and any future attempt to reproduce it must come at the BMC differently.
data contention_does_not_produce_the_state: NonEmptyStr = "the harness's own sol deactivate evicts a competing holder, so occupying the console does not blind it; that run hit the firmware-error path instead and retried, correctly"

// ── the reading that did produce it ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// AN UNANSWERABLE CONTROLLER LOOKS EXACTLY LIKE A DROPPED CONSOLE FROM THE HARNESS'S SIDE: no loader
// banner, no firmware error, an empty capture. That is the same reading, arrived at by a different
// cause, and it is the reading the arm is written against.
//
// THE ARM FIRED ON THE FIRST ATTEMPT AND STOPPED. Exit 3, one attempt, no second power cycle -- where
// the previous harness would have cycled up to four times.
data observed_refusal: List<NonEmptyStr> = [
  "attempt 1 produced no loader banner and no firmware error",
  "the harness refused rather than cycling, and named the capture to inspect",
  "exit 3, one attempt: the arm that previously cycled four times stopped at the first ambiguity",
]

// ── two things about the setup that were not careful ───────────────────────────────────────────

// THE ADDRESS WAS CHOSEN WITHOUT CHECKING IT WAS FREE, and something lives there. Its MAC is on the
// segment and it answers ping. The IPMI commands failed with "Unable to establish IPMI v2 / RMCP+
// session", so no power command reached it -- but that was the credentials failing, not care taken
// beforehand. Picking an address for a destructive-command harness without first establishing that
// nothing is on it is the same shape as the reading this arm exists to refuse.
data setup_defect: NonEmptyStr = "192.168.1.199 was assumed free and is not; a device answers there. No IPMI session was established so no power command landed, which was luck rather than a check"

// AND THE CAUSE UNDER TEST IS NOT THE CAUSE THAT BIT US. The original incident was a console the BMC
// closed mid-run; this exercise produced an unanswerable controller instead. The harness cannot tell
// them apart, which is precisely why the arm is written against the READING rather than the cause --
// but the receipt should not claim the original failure was reproduced, because it was not.
data what_was_not_reproduced: NonEmptyStr = "a BMC-closed console during a live run was not reproduced; an equivalent READING was produced by a different cause, and the arm keys on the reading"

// ── standing ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

data arm_standing: NonEmptyStr = "exercised: the ambiguous-reading refusal fires and stops, proven against a real harness run rather than asserted"

data still_unexercised: List<NonEmptyStr> = [
  "the BMC-closed-console cause specifically, which cannot be produced by contention and has no known trigger",
  "a release that fails or returns ambiguously, which remains the oldest unexercised arm in this lane",
]
